  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "UTZOx/gs2Kmz0r1dSJeXYmN7ROQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:29:40.001Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Stripped of the stale API names, the spec's goal list is the clearest statement of *why* relational processors have a base class at all:\n\n- a **unified interface** for processors that need persistent relational storage;\n- **type-safe** database operations;\n- **migration management standardised at the project level**, not per processor;\n- **less boilerplate** \u2014 the author writes business logic, not database setup;\n- **consistent error handling and resource management**;\n- **multiple processors sharing database tables within a project** \u2014 an explicit goal, which is why namespacing is a first-class concern rather than a convention;\n- automatic schema generation and migration; a clear split between infrastructure and business logic; easy testing and debugging of processor logic.\n\nThe problem it names is worth quoting in spirit: before it, processor implementations were inconsistent, database setup was manual, and there was no type safety.\n\nTwo design consequences that did survive into the shipped code: the **namespace** is part of the processor's identity (the generated template overrides `static getNamespace(driveId)` with the default `` `${this.name}_${driveId.replaceAll(\"-\", \"_\")}` ``, which is how two processors can share a database without colliding), and migrations live with the processor as an `up` / `down` pair rather than in a central migration folder.\n\n## Provenance\n- `docs/specs/relational-db-processor.md` \u2014 Goals (Primary and Supporting); Database Abstractions \u203a Migration Management\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:29:40.001Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:29:40.001Z"
    }
  },
